# =============================================================================
#  UNIVERSAL HEATSOAK MACRO  —  Heatsoak_Universal.cfg
# =============================================================================
#
#  DEPLOYED ON: Boss (SV08 toolchanger), 2026-09-14 -- template's generic entry point
#  (originally PRINT_START) renamed to START_PRINT to match this printer's existing slicer
#  profile. No START_PRINT_BASE/MMU
#  sections here: PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK does Boss's rough-QGL + tool-offset-calibration pass
#  (ported from the old HEATSOAK macro, TC_CALIBRATION_MODE gating untouched), and both
#  post-soak/no-soak paths call straight into Boss's existing _START_PRINT (sovol-macros.cfg,
#  unmodified except for an added toolchanger safety check at its top -- see that file).
#  PAUSE/RESUME/CANCEL_PRINT all live in sovol-macros.cfg as single canonical macros -- see
#  the comments on those sections for why (two same-named [gcode_macro] sections silently
#  merge in Klipper instead of erroring; this bit RESUME for real before this deploy).
#
#  SLICER START GCODE: unchanged from before this deploy -- the Orca profile still calls
#  START_PRINT with whatever BED_TEMP/EXTRUDER_TEMP/TOOL/TOOL_TEMP/T{n}_TEMP/CALIBRATE fields
#  it already sends (see _START_PRINT's own header comment in sovol-macros.cfg for the full
#  list) -- not independently re-verified against the live Orca profile itself here.
#
#  FEATURES
#  --------
#  * Soak time scales with print duration parsed from filename (1h30m etc.)
#    Falls back to soak_default_minutes if no time code found in filename.
#  * Countdown heartbeat doesn't start until the bed has actually reached
#    target temp (and nozzle/park are done) -- not from the moment the soak
#    begins. Reuses M190's own blocking wait rather than polling a threshold.
#  * Optional chamber sensor: waits for temp before countdown begins,
#    and/or skips soak if chamber is already close enough to target.
#    Chamber target comes from the CHAMBER= param passed by slicer.
#    Uses fuzzy suffix matching -- no need for exact sensor key.
#  * Multiple fans at individual speeds during soak; all zeroed on completion.
#  * Popup appears BEFORE machine starts moving (prep fires via delayed_gcode).
#  * Shows largest part footprint area when exclude_object is available.
#  * Real SVG build-plate layout (every part's outline) sent to the
#    Console tab (Mainsail/Fluidd) when exclude_object data is available --
#    separate from the popup, which can't render markup.
#  * Skip / Adjust / Cancel buttons in Mainsail / Fluidd / KlipperScreen.
#  * heatsoak_active flag prevents double-fire on SKIP or stale TICK.
#  * CANCEL restores gcode state stack so next print starts clean.
#  * Adaptive tick interval (5 min -> 1 min -> 10 s -> 5 s).
#  * A REAL blocking pause (auto-detected BASE_PAUSE/PAUSE_BASE and
#    BASE_RESUME/RESUME_BASE) actually halts the print queue during the
#    soak -- see the INIT branch below for why this matters.
#  * Stale heatsoak_active state from before a Klipper restart is
#    automatically cleared on startup.
#  * An external CANCEL_PRINT (Mainsail's button, a raw API call -- anything
#    other than this macro's own Cancel button) also tears down any active
#    soak cleanly instead of leaving its timers running unaware.
#
# =============================================================================
#
# =============================================================================
#  CHANGELOG
# =============================================================================
#  2026-08-04/05 -- Two real bugs found and fixed while deploying this on the
#  Voron 0 (full story in the MMU SUPPORT SECTION note near the bottom):
#    1. get_duration_from_name's regex lacked a negative lookahead, so
#       filenames like "3dbenchy...0.25mm...2h20m.gcode" misread "3d" as
#       3 days and "25m" out of "0.25mm" as 25 minutes -- inflated an
#       8-minute print to "146h 45m" and always clamped to the 45-min
#       ceiling. Fixed: (?![a-zA-Z]) restored, case-sensitivity restored
#       (dropped the |lower).
#    2. Storing the slicer's raw {rawparams} string in `state` and later
#       re-serializing the whole dict via SET_GCODE_VARIABLE ... VALUE=
#       "{state}" breaks if any param value contains embedded double quotes
#       (e.g. a slicer passing SURFACE="High Temp Plate") -- the outer
#       quoting collides and Klipper logs "Malformed command", silently
#       never saving state. Fixed: store the already-decoded named fields
#       (LAYER/FILAMENT/EXCLUDE/SURFACE/OAPA/DMGCC, or whatever your slicer
#       passes) instead of the raw string, and re-quote fresh at the
#       START_PRINT_BASE call site in resume_and_start().
#
#  2026-08-06 -- Standing requirement added: homing (Z at minimum), leveling/
#  QGL, and bed mesh must ALWAYS happen hot, after the soak -- see the
#  "Re-home, level, mesh" comment block in START_PRINT_BASE below for the
#  full rationale and a concrete gotcha (a leveling module's "already
#  applied" flag silently skipping a hot re-level because a pre-soak homing
#  pass had already flipped it true) hit for real on the Rat (RatOS).
#
#  2026-08-27 -- Pause/resume macro name (BASE_PAUSE vs PAUSE_BASE,
#  BASE_RESUME vs RESUME_BASE) is now auto-detected at runtime via
#  _resolve_pause_macro()/_resolve_resume_macro(), instead of being
#  hardcoded and requiring manual per-printer verification before deploying.
#  Hard-errors via action_raise_error if no rename_existing is configured,
#  rather than risking the old silent "Unknown command" failure mode. Also
#  fixed: the soak-adjust popup buttons (+15/+10/+5/-5/-10/-15) rendered in a
#  3-per-row grid with mismatched +/- pairs in the outer two columns --
#  reordered so each column is a matched pair.
#
#  2026-08-27 (later same day) -- The resolver above was itself broken on
#  first deploy: `printer["gcode_macro BASE_PAUSE"] is defined` can never be
#  true for a rename_existing target derived from PAUSE/RESUME, since those
#  are backed by pause_resume.py, not a [gcode_macro X] section -- confirmed
#  live when a real HEATSOAK call on the Voron 0 hard-errored even though
#  BASE_PAUSE was a freshly-confirmed-registered real command. Fixed by
#  reading printer.configfile.settings["gcode_macro pause"/"resume"].
#  rename_existing directly instead of probing for a status object.
#  Pause-side fix live-confirmed on the Voron 0 via a real HEATSOAK call;
#  resume-side fix applied the same way but not yet separately live-tested
#  (Voron 0's own resume path diverged for MMU reasons before this fix, so
#  it never exercised _resolve_resume_macro at all).
#
#  2026-08-27 (same day, beta) -- Countdown heartbeat (_HEATSOAK_UPDATE) used
#  to be scheduled from INIT, in parallel with _HEATSOAK_PREP's bed/nozzle
#  wait, not after it. Klipper's reactor keeps pumping other delayed_gcode
#  timers while a blocking wait like M190 is paused, so soak_left could
#  count all the way down -- and resume_and_start() actually RESUME the
#  print -- before the bed had even reached target temp, let alone before
#  nozzle-heat/park ran. Not just cosmetic: a short enough soak_sec on a
#  slow-heating bed could resume mid-heat. Fixed by moving the first
#  UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE call out of INIT and into the end
#  of [delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_PREP], right after PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK --
#  the visible countdown (and the resume it eventually triggers) now cannot
#  start until bed+nozzle+park are actually done.
#
#  2026-08-27 (later still) -- SVG build-plate layout added, after checking
#  Contomo's actual source (github.com/Contomo/klipper-questionable-macros)
#  instead of guessing: his PART_AREA macro sends a real <svg> string via a
#  BARE action_respond_info() call, not through the action:prompt_* popup
#  protocol -- that's a different, text/button-only channel that can't carry
#  markup at all. Ported as draw_buildplate_layout_svg(), called once per
#  soak (not per tick) right after the popup first shows. Renders in the
#  Console tab, not the popup -- not yet live-tested on real hardware.
#
#  2026-08-31 (beta, not yet print-tested) -- The popup used to show a
#  static "Soak remaining: Xm" the whole time the bed/nozzle were heating up
#  (since the real countdown heartbeat, by design, doesn't start until
#  heating is done -- see the 2026-08-27 entry above), which reads as
#  frozen/stuck on a slow-heating bed. Added a state.heating flag (true at
#  INIT, flipped false once bed+nozzle are actually reached) so show_ui()
#  can branch to a live "Bed: Xc / Yc" (and "Nozzle: Xc / Yc" once
#  cfg.soak_nozzle_temp > 0) display during heat-up, then switch back to
#  the normal "Soak remaining" countdown once heating finishes.
#
#  First attempt (same day) used M190/M109 unchanged for the actual wait,
#  plus a SEPARATE delayed_gcode heartbeat (_HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK) meant to
#  tick every 3s DURING that wait and redraw the popup. Live-tested on SV0
#  and found completely broken -- zero popup updates the entire ~7-minute
#  bed heat-up. Root cause, confirmed by reading klippy/gcode.py and
#  klippy/reactor.py directly: Klipper serializes ALL gcode script
#  execution through a single mutex (GCodeDispatch.run_script()'s
#  `with self.mutex:`), held for the ENTIRE duration of a blocking call
#  like M190/M109 -- not released between the reactor.pause() cycles
#  inside M190's own wait loop. A separate delayed_gcode's TIMER can still
#  fire during that wait (that part of the 2026-08-27 comment was right),
#  but the gcode it tries to run (ReactorMutex.__enter__, which queues the
#  current greenlet and reactor.pause()s until woken by __exit__) blocks
#  waiting for that same mutex and doesn't actually execute until the
#  blocking script finishes and releases it -- by which point
#  state.heating had already been flipped false by _HEATSOAK_PREP itself,
#  so the queued tick saw that and silently no-op'd. Nothing was
#  physically stuck (M190/M109 completed normally on their own, unaffected
#  by the broken heartbeat), just the popup never visibly updated.
#
#  Real fix: replaced the blocking M190/M109 in _HEATSOAK_PREP with a
#  non-blocking M140/M104 plus a POLLING heartbeat (still named
#  _HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK) that checks printer.heater_bed.temperature /
#  printer.extruder.temperature against target on each of its own
#  independent ticks (every 2s), updates the popup, and re-arms itself
#  until both are within tolerance (1c bed / 2c nozzle -- intentionally
#  looser than Klipper's own PID-settle wait, which also checks the rate
#  of change, not just the value; close enough for a pre-print soak
#  target). Once both are reached, that same tick parks, flips
#  state.heating false, and starts the real countdown heartbeat --
#  replacing what M190/M109/PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK used to do inline in
#  _HEATSOAK_PREP. Each poll tick is its own independent, always-completing
#  script (no nested blocking call), so it never contends with the mutex
#  the way the first attempt did. Also fixed a related latent bug found
#  while adding the heating-flag concept: the ADJUST (+/- minute buttons)
#  handler unconditionally armed _HEATSOAK_UPDATE (DURATION=0.2) on every
#  click, including during the heating phase -- which would have
#  re-triggered the exact "countdown starts before the bed is at temp" bug
#  from the 2026-08-27 fix if a user ever hit Adjust before heating
#  finished. Now guarded behind `not state.get("heating", false)`.
#
#  Verified by parsing every gcode: block with a local Jinja env
#  replicating Klipper's exact config (single-brace delimiters plus
#  Klipper's own #-strips-to-newline raw preprocessing) -- no
#  TemplateSyntaxErrors. The mutex root-cause finding itself was verified
#  by reading the live klippy/gcode.py and klippy/reactor.py source on
#  SV0's own Klipper install directly (not assumed from memory), and by
#  checking SV0's real klippy.log/gcode_store for the test run that
#  exposed the first attempt's bug. The corrected poll-based version is
#  NOT yet print-tested on real hardware -- pushed to a `beta` branch on
#  GitHub, not main, pending real prints.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
#  PRINTER CONFIGURATION  --  edit everything in this section
# =============================================================================

[gcode_macro _HEATSOAK_CFG]
description: Central configuration -- edit here, not in the logic macros

# -- Chamber sensor -----------------------------------------------------------
# Suffix of your chamber sensor name as it appears in printer.cfg.
# Matching is case-insensitive suffix -- so "chamber_temp", "chamber", or
# "CHAMBER_TEMP" all work without needing the full "temperature_sensor ..." key.
# Set to "" to disable all chamber logic entirely.
variable_chamber_sensor: ""

# Countdown holds until chamber reaches the CHAMBER temp passed by the slicer.
# Set to 0 to disable (default: off).
variable_chamber_wait_temp: 0.0

# If the chamber is already within this many degrees of the CHAMBER target
# passed by the slicer, skip the soak entirely (printer already hot from a
# previous print). e.g. 5 means: if CHAMBER=45 and chamber reads >=40 skip.
# Requires slicer to pass CHAMBER= -- if it does not, this check is skipped.
# Set to 0 to disable.
variable_skip_if_chamber_within: 0.0

# -- Soak time calculation ----------------------------------------------------
# Seconds to soak per minute of estimated print time.
# e.g. 5 -> 60 min print = 5 min soak, 30 min print = 2.5 min soak
variable_soak_sec_per_print_min: 5.0

# If no time code is found in the filename, assume this many minutes of print time.
variable_soak_default_minutes: 5.0

# Hard floor and ceiling on soak time (seconds).
variable_soak_min_seconds: 300.0
variable_soak_max_seconds: 2700.0

# -- Trigger conditions -------------------------------------------------------
# Soak is SKIPPED entirely when bed temp OR print duration is below these:
variable_min_bed_temp_to_trigger: 55.0
variable_min_print_minutes_to_trigger: 5.0

# -- Nozzle temperature during soak ------------------------------------------
# Low enough to avoid ooze, high enough for a probe that needs some heat.
# Set to 0 to leave nozzle cold during soak.
# Boss-specific: matches TC_SERVICE_TOOL.probe_temp (sovol-macros.cfg) -- the service
# tool's safe probing/QGL/calibration temp, same value the old HEATSOAK macro used.
variable_soak_nozzle_temp: 125.0

# -- Fans during soak ---------------------------------------------------------
# The part cooling fan ([fan] type) does NOT support SET_FAN_SPEED and must be
# controlled separately via M106/M107.
# Speed 0.0-1.0. Set to 0.0 to leave the part fan off during soak.
# Boss-specific: 1.0 matches the old HEATSOAK macro's M106 S255 (blow hot air during soak).
variable_soak_part_fan_speed: 1.0

# [fan_generic] fans only -- use SET_FAN_SPEED. Fan names must match exactly as
# defined in printer.cfg. Speed is 0.0-1.0. All listed fans are zeroed when
# soak ends -- START_PRINT_BASE takes over fan control for the print.
# Set to [] to leave all generic fans untouched during soak.
# On enclosed printers, running circulation fans during soak helps distribute
# heat evenly through the chamber and speeds up thermal stabilisation.
# Examples:
#   Nevermore filter: [["nevermore", 1.0]]
#   SV0-style:        [["fan2", 0.5], ["fan3", 0.0]]
#   Multiple:         [["nevermore", 1.0], ["exhaust", 0.0]]
# NOTE: Klipper requires this to be on a single line.
variable_soak_fans: []

# -- Park position ------------------------------------------------------------
# Z height to park at during soak.
variable_park_z: 10.0

# -- Heat-up popup refresh -----------------------------------------------------
# How often (seconds) the popup redraws with live bed/nozzle temp while
# heating up, before the real soak countdown starts. Each redraw resends a
# full action:prompt_begin/text/show sequence (Mainsail/Fluidd's popup
# protocol has no partial-update command), which rebuilds the whole dialog
# every time -- too short and it visibly flashes/disappears faster than you
# can read the new number (found live: 2s and 4s both too fast, 30s good).
variable_heatup_tick_interval: 30.0

gcode:
    # Config-only macro -- never call directly.


# =============================================================================
#  USER HOOK: PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK
#  Move toolhead to soak position after homing.
#  Default: bed centre at park_z. Override to customise.
# =============================================================================

[gcode_macro PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK]
description: "Park the toolhead for heatsoak. Boss-specific: also does the rough QGL +\
    tool-offset calibration (or brush-only) pass here, ported from the old HEATSOAK macro's\
    steps 3-6 -- this runs once, right as heating finishes and before the visible countdown\
    starts ticking (soak_left isn't decremented until after this returns), same timing the old\
    HEATSOAK macro used. TC_CALIBRATION_MODE gating is untouched, per explicit confirmation\
    this is intentional -- do not flip its default."
gcode:
    {% set cfg = printer["gcode_macro _HEATSOAK_CFG"] %}
    {% set st  = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"] %}
    {% set svc = printer["gcode_macro TC_SERVICE_TOOL"] %}
    {% set svc_tool = svc.service_tool|int %}

    # Force baseline physical service tool onto the gantry before QGL/calibration.
    SELECT_TOOL T={svc_tool}

    # Rough QGL with the service tool -- same as old HEATSOAK step 3.
    M117 Initial QGL (rough)...
    QUAD_GANTRY_LEVEL

    # Tool offset calibration (or brush-only), gated by TC_CALIBRATION_MODE -- unchanged
    # from the old HEATSOAK macro. Runs here so it's still part of the heatsoak session
    # (before the countdown proper), not moved out to post-soak print-start.
    {% set do_cal = st.state.get("calibrate", 1)|int %}
    {% set cal_enabled = 1 %}
    {% if "gcode_macro TC_CALIBRATION_MODE" in printer %}
        {% set cal_enabled = printer["gcode_macro TC_CALIBRATION_MODE"].enabled|int %}
    {% endif %}

    {% if do_cal and cal_enabled %}
        M117 Calibrating tool offsets...
        CALIBRATE_TOOL_OFFSETS CAL_TEMP={cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|int}
    {% else %}
        {% if do_cal and not cal_enabled %}
            { action_respond_info("HEATSOAK: calibration disabled via TC_CALIBRATION_MODE; brushing service tool only") }
        {% else %}
            { action_respond_info("HEATSOAK: calibration skipped (CALIBRATE=" ~ do_cal ~ "); brushing service tool only") }
        {% endif %}

        M117 Brushing service tool T{svc_tool}...
        SELECT_TOOL T={svc_tool}
        CALIBRATE_MOVE_OVER_PROBE
        KOMB
        M104 T{svc_tool} S{cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|int}
    {% endif %}

    # Default center park at soak height -- unchanged from the template.
    {% if printer.toolhead.homed_axes|lower == "xyz" %}
        {% set cx = (printer.toolhead.axis_maximum.x|float) / 2.0 %}
        {% set cy = (printer.toolhead.axis_maximum.y|float) / 2.0 %}
        G90
        G1 Z{cfg.park_z} F1800
        G1 X{cx} Y{cy} F6000
    {% endif %}


# =============================================================================
#  STARTUP CLEANUP
#  Any heatsoak_active/state left over from before a restart is guaranteed
#  stale: the delayed_gcode timer chain (_HEATSOAK_UPDATE/_HEATSOAK_PREP) that
#  drives a soak's countdown does NOT survive a Klipper restart, so an
#  "active" session found at startup can never be a real, continuable soak --
#  it's orphaned state from whatever interrupted the previous session
#  (crash, manual restart) before its own cleanup ran.
# =============================================================================

[delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_STARTUP_CLEAR]
initial_duration: 2
gcode:
    {% set st = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"] %}
    {% if st.heatsoak_active or (st.state and (st.state|string) != "{}") %}
        RESPOND TYPE=echo MSG="Heatsoak: clearing stale active state left over from before this restart"
        SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=heatsoak_active VALUE=0
        SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{{}}"
    {% endif %}


# =============================================================================
#  PHYSICAL PREP  --  fires via delayed_gcode so popup appears first
# =============================================================================

[delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_PREP]
gcode:
    {% set st  = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"] %}
    {% set cfg = printer["gcode_macro _HEATSOAK_CFG"] %}

    {% if st.state and (st.state|string) != "{}" and st.heatsoak_active %}
        {% set bed = st.state.get("bed", 60)|float %}

        # Start bed AND nozzle heating together, both non-blocking -- calibration only
        # needs the nozzle, not the bed, so these must not be sequenced (see
        # PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK / the HEATUP TICK BRANCH below for why bed and
        # nozzle-readiness are now checked independently instead of bed-then-nozzle).
        M140 S{bed}
        {% if cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|float > 0 %}
            M104 S{cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|int}
        {% endif %}

        {% set soak_timeout = (cfg.soak_max_seconds|float + 600)|int %}
        SET_IDLE_TIMEOUT TIMEOUT={soak_timeout}

        # Set generic fans (fan_generic type -- SET_FAN_SPEED)
        {% for fan_entry in cfg.soak_fans %}
            SET_FAN_SPEED FAN={fan_entry[0]} SPEED={fan_entry[1]}
        {% endfor %}

        # Part cooling fan (fan type -- M106/M107 only)
        {% if cfg.soak_part_fan_speed|float > 0 %}
            M106 S{(cfg.soak_part_fan_speed|float * 255)|int}
        {% else %}
            M107
        {% endif %}

        # Home if needed
        {% if printer.toolhead.homed_axes|lower != "xyz" %}
            G28
        {% endif %}

        # Bed/nozzle wait is a non-blocking POLL heartbeat, not M190/M109 --
        # see the 2026-08-31 CHANGELOG entry for why: Klipper serializes ALL
        # gcode script execution through a single mutex
        # (klippy/gcode.py GCodeDispatch.run_script()'s `with self.mutex:`,
        # held for the whole duration of a blocking call like M190/M109).
        # A separate delayed_gcode's TIMER can still fire during that wait,
        # but the gcode it tries to run queues on that same mutex and
        # doesn't actually execute until the blocking script finishes --
        # so a heartbeat trying to update the popup DURING M190 is silently
        # deferred until AFTER it, making it useless for live progress.
        # Confirmed by reading klippy/reactor.py's ReactorMutex directly,
        # and live: the popup never updated once during a real ~7-minute
        # bed heat-up test (zero prompt messages logged the whole window).
        # Polling instead sidesteps the mutex entirely -- each tick is its
        # own independent, always-completing script, so it can safely show
        # live temps and re-arm itself. See the HEATUP TICK BRANCH below,
        # which owns the actual wait/park/countdown-handoff now.
        UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK DURATION=1
    {% endif %}


# =============================================================================
#  COUNTDOWN HEARTBEAT
# =============================================================================

[delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_UPDATE]
gcode:
    UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=0
    {% set st = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"] %}
    {% if st.state and (st.state|string) != "{}" and st.heatsoak_active %}
        START_PRINT TICK=1
    {% endif %}


# =============================================================================
#  HEATING PROGRESS HEARTBEAT
#  Ticks while the bed/nozzle are still heating, before the real soak
#  countdown starts, so the popup shows live temp progress instead of a
#  frozen "Soak remaining" value that looks stuck for however long heating
#  takes. Self-cancels once _HEATSOAK_PREP flips state.heating to false.
# =============================================================================

[delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK]
gcode:
    UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK DURATION=0
    {% set st = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"] %}
    {% if st.state and (st.state|string) != "{}" and st.heatsoak_active %}
        START_PRINT HEATUP_TICK=1
    {% endif %}


# =============================================================================
#  START_PRINT  --  main entry point called by slicer
#  (rename this + all internal self-references to match your slicer profile
#  if it calls something else, e.g. HEATSOAK -- see the Voron 0 deployment
#  for an example of a pure rename with no logic changes)
#
#  Slicer call:
#    START_PRINT BED={bed_temperature} EXTRUDER={temperature}
#
#  Internal calls (do not use manually):
#    START_PRINT TICK=1        -- heartbeat tick
#    START_PRINT SKIP=1        -- user pressed Skip
#    START_PRINT CANCEL=1      -- user pressed Cancel
#    START_PRINT ADJUST=<+/->  -- user adjusted time (minutes)
#
# =============================================================================

[gcode_macro START_PRINT]
variable_state: {}
variable_heatsoak_active: 0

gcode:
    {% set SELF      = "START_PRINT" %}
    {% set IS_TICK   = params.TICK|default(0)|int %}
    {% set IS_SKIP   = params.SKIP|default(0)|int %}
    {% set IS_CANCEL = params.CANCEL|default(0)|int %}
    {% set IS_ADJUST = "ADJUST" in params %}
    {% set IS_HEATUP_TICK = params.HEATUP_TICK|default(0)|int %}
    {% set IS_INIT   = not IS_TICK and not IS_SKIP and not IS_CANCEL and not IS_ADJUST and not IS_HEATUP_TICK %}

    {% set cfg    = printer["gcode_macro _HEATSOAK_CFG"] %}
    {% set state  = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"].state %}
    {% set active = printer["gcode_macro START_PRINT"].heatsoak_active %}

    # ── Shared Jinja helpers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    {%- macro format_time(sec) -%}
        {%- set s  = sec|int(0) -%}
        {%- set h  = s // 3600 -%}
        {%- set m  = (s % 3600) // 60 -%}
        {%- set s2 = s % 60 -%}
        {%- set parts = [] -%}
        {%- if h -%}{%- set _ = parts.append(h ~ "h") -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- if m -%}{%- set _ = parts.append(m ~ "m") -%}{%- endif -%}
        {%- set _ = parts.append(s2 ~ "s") -%}
        { parts[0:2]|join(" ") }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro get_chamber_temp(sensor_suffix) -%}
        {%- set result = [] -%}
        {%- if sensor_suffix|trim -%}
            {%- for h in printer.heaters.available_sensors -%}
                {%- if (h|lower).endswith(sensor_suffix|trim|lower) and not result -%}
                    {%- set _ = result.append(printer[h].temperature) -%}
                {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        { result[0] if result else "" }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    # NOTE: the comment explaining the (?![a-zA-Z]) lookahead below is
    # deliberately OUTSIDE the macro body (before {%- macro -%}, not inside
    # it) -- any literal text inside a Jinja {%- macro -%}...{%- endmacro -%}
    # block becomes part of that macro's returned value, which would corrupt
    # its numeric result. See CHANGELOG at the top of this file for why this
    # lookahead matters: without it, "3dbenchy...0.25mm...2h20m.gcode" reads
    # "3d" as 3 days and "25m" (from "0.25mm") as 25 minutes.
    {%- macro get_duration_from_name(fname) -%}
        {%- set glob     = printer.printer.__class__.__init__.__globals__ -%}
        {%- set re       = glob.importlib.import_module("re") -%}
        {%- set unit_map = {"d": 86400, "h": 3600, "m": 60, "s": 1} -%}
        {%- set matches  = re.findall("([0-9]+)([dhms])(?![a-zA-Z])", fname|string) -%}
        {%- set durations = [] -%}
        {%- for num, unit in matches if unit in unit_map -%}
            {%- set _ = durations.append(num|float * unit_map[unit]) -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        { durations|sum if durations else -1 }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro polygon_area(poly) -%}
        {%- set n  = poly|length -%}
        {%- set ns = namespace(acc=0) -%}
        {%- for point in poly -%}
            {%- set j      = (loop.index0 + 1) % n -%}
            {%- set ns.acc = ns.acc + (point[0] * poly[j][1] - poly[j][0] * point[1]) -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        { (ns.acc / 2.0)|abs }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro max_polygon_area(objs) -%}
        {%- set areas = [] -%}
        {%- for p in objs|map(attribute="polygon") -%}
            {%- set _ = areas.append(polygon_area(p)) -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        { areas|map("float")|list|max if areas else 0 }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    # Renders a real SVG outline of every excluded-object polygon on the bed
    # and sends it as a BARE action_respond_info() call -- deliberately NOT
    # wrapped in the ui()/action:prompt_ helper below, which is a separate,
    # text/button-only protocol that cannot carry markup. Ported from
    # Contomo's original (github.com/Contomo/klipper-questionable-macros,
    # macro-examples/interruptable_heatsoak_print_start.cfg, PART_AREA
    # macro) after checking his real source instead of guessing -- his
    # version does exactly this (raw SVG string straight into a bare
    # action_respond_info), which Mainsail's/Fluidd's console tab renders
    # inline. This is a different UI surface than the popup dialog (which
    # only speaks action:prompt_* and genuinely cannot show it) -- open the
    # Console tab, not the popup, to see it. Called once per soak, not on
    # every tick, since the layout doesn't change mid-soak.
    #
    # NEVER use hex colors (#rrggbb) or any other literal '#' anywhere in
    # this macro's SVG string output, or anywhere else in a gcode_macro body
    # in ANY .cfg Klipper loads -- confirmed live via a real deploy failure:
    # Klipper's config-file reader (configfile.py's ConfigFileReader,
    # both append_fileconfig() and _parse_config()) does
    # `pos = line.find('#'); line = line[:pos]` on EVERY raw line of the
    # file, UNCONDITIONALLY, before configparser or Jinja ever see it -- with
    # zero awareness of quoting. A hex color inside a quoted string gets
    # silently truncated mid-line along with everything after it, corrupting
    # the template and producing a confusing downstream Jinja
    # TemplateSyntaxError (e.g. "expected token ',', got 'x'") that points
    # nowhere near the real cause. This is exactly why Contomo's original
    # PART_AREA macro uses named CSS colors ('cyan'/'gray'/'black'/'white')
    # instead of hex -- not a style choice, a hard requirement. Named colors
    # only, here and in any future edit to this file.
    {%- macro draw_buildplate_layout_svg(objs, w, h, pad=10) -%}
        {%- set total_w = (w + pad * 2) -%}
        {%- set total_h = (h + pad * 2) -%}
        {%- set svg_parts = [] -%}
        {%- set _ = svg_parts.append('<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="' ~ total_w|round(0) ~ '" height="' ~ total_h|round(0) ~ '" viewBox="0 0 ' ~ total_w|round(0) ~ ' ' ~ total_h|round(0) ~ '" style="background-color:black;">') -%}
        {%- set _ = svg_parts.append('<rect x="' ~ pad ~ '" y="' ~ pad ~ '" width="' ~ w ~ '" height="' ~ h ~ '" fill="none" stroke="white" stroke-width="1" />') -%}
        {%- for obj in objs -%}
            {%- set pts = [] -%}
            {%- for p in obj.polygon -%}
                {%- set xv = (p[0] + pad)|round(2) -%}
                {%- set yv = (h - p[1] + pad)|round(2) -%}
                {%- set _ = pts.append(xv|string ~ "," ~ yv|string) -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
            {%- set _ = svg_parts.append('<polygon points="' ~ pts|join(" ") ~ '" stroke="cyan" fill="gray" stroke-width="1" />') -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
        {%- set _ = svg_parts.append("</svg>") -%}
        { svg_parts|join("") }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro ui(msg)  -%}{ action_respond_info("action:prompt_" ~ msg) }{%- endmacro -%}
    {%- macro add(msg) -%}{ ui(msg) }{ ui("show") }{%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro show_ui(soak_left) -%}
        {% set ps = printer.print_stats %}
        { ui("begin Heatsoak -- " ~ (ps.filename.split("_")[0]|default("next print"))) }
        { ui("text Print file: " ~ (ps.filename|default("unknown")|string)) }
        {% set pdur = state.get("print_dur_sec", 0)|int %}
        {% if pdur > 0 %}
            { ui("text Print duration: " ~ format_time(pdur)) }
        {% else %}
            { ui("text Print duration: Unknown") }
        {% endif %}
        {% if state.get("heating", false) %}
            { ui("text Bed: " ~ (printer.heater_bed.temperature|round(1)) ~ "C / " ~ (state.get("bed", 0)|int) ~ "C") }
            {% if cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|float > 0 %}
                { ui("text Nozzle: " ~ (printer.extruder.temperature|round(1)) ~ "C / " ~ (cfg.soak_nozzle_temp|int) ~ "C") }
            {% endif %}
            { ui("text Soak: " ~ format_time(soak_left|int) ~ " once heated" ~ (" (calibration done)" if state.get("calibrated", false) else " (calibrating tools now)")) }
        {% else %}
            { ui("text Soak remaining: " ~ format_time(soak_left|int)) }
            { ui("text Bed target: " ~ (state.get("bed", 0)|int)|string ~ "C") }
            { ui("text Nozzle standby: " ~ (state.get("noz", 0)|int)|string ~ "C") }
        {% endif %}
        {% if cfg.chamber_sensor|trim %}
            {% set ctemp = get_chamber_temp(cfg.chamber_sensor) %}
            {% if ctemp %}
                {% set chamber_target = state.get("chamber_target", 0)|float %}
                {% if chamber_target > 0 %}
                    { ui("text Chamber: " ~ (ctemp|float)|round(1)|string ~ "C / " ~ chamber_target|int|string ~ "C target") }
                {% else %}
                    { ui("text Chamber: " ~ (ctemp|float)|round(1)|string ~ "C") }
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endif %}
        {% if printer.exclude_object is defined and printer.exclude_object.objects %}
            {% set area_cm2 = (max_polygon_area(printer.exclude_object.objects)|float / 100.0)|int %}
            { ui("text Largest part area: " ~ area_cm2|string ~ " cm2") }
        {% endif %}
        { ui("button_group_start") }
        {% for step in [15, 10, 5, -15, -10, -5] %}
            { ui("button " ~ ("+" ~ step|string if step > 0 else step|string) ~ " min|" ~ SELF ~ " ADJUST=" ~ step ~ "|accent--text text--" ~ ("lighten" if step > 0 else "darken") ~ "-3") }
        {% endfor %}
        { ui("button_group_end") }
        { ui("footer_button [ X ] Cancel|" ~ SELF ~ " CANCEL=1|error") }
        { add("footer_button [ > ] Skip|" ~ SELF ~ " SKIP=1|success") }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro zero_soak_fans() -%}
        {% for fan_entry in cfg.soak_fans %}
            SET_FAN_SPEED FAN={fan_entry[0]} SPEED=0
        {% endfor %}
        M107
    {%- endmacro -%}

    # Soak time itself is tracked as an ABSOLUTE deadline (state.soak_deadline, in
    # toolhead.estimated_print_time seconds -- a real wall-clock-derived reading that keeps
    # advancing through PAUSE/heating/calibration, unlike print_stats.total_duration which
    # freezes while paused) rather than a decrementing counter -- but the deadline is only SET
    # once the bed actually reaches target temp (see the HEATUP TICK BRANCH below), not at
    # INIT. Soaking is specifically about holding the frame/gantry hot once the bed is at
    # temperature, so the soak duration itself (state.soak_total, the raw seconds decided at
    # INIT) always runs in full starting from that moment -- bed heat-up time is never
    # subtracted from it. Tool calibration (PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK) runs immediately AFTER the
    # deadline is set, in the same tick, rather than gating the countdown behind it -- so
    # however long calibration takes IS counted against the already-running deadline (a real
    # timestamp, unaffected by whether the popup could redraw during that blocking call --
    # see the HEATUP TICK BRANCH's own comment for why it can't). If calibration alone runs
    # past the deadline, remaining() reads 0 immediately once it returns and the branch
    # resumes right there, no separate countdown wait tacked on afterward. remaining() is
    # only meaningful once soak_deadline exists (i.e. once the bed is ready); before that,
    # callers show state.soak_total directly (a fixed value, not counting down).
    {%- macro remaining() -%}
        { [state.get("soak_deadline", 0)|float - printer.toolhead.estimated_print_time|float, 0.0]|max }
    {%- endmacro -%}

    # 2026-08-27 (beta), rewritten after a live test caught a real bug:
    # checking `printer["gcode_macro BASE_PAUSE"] is defined` NEVER works for
    # PAUSE/RESUME, because rename_existing on a command backed by
    # pause_resume.py (not a plain [gcode_macro X] section) registers the new
    # name as a callable gcode command but does NOT create a queryable
    # `gcode_macro <name>` status object -- confirmed live: /printer/objects/
    # list never listed gcode_macro BASE_PAUSE even though /printer/gcode/
    # help showed it as a real command, and a real HEATSOAK call hard-errored
    # on this exact check every time. Read the CONFIGURED rename_existing
    # value directly instead, via printer.configfile.settings -- confirmed
    # live this returns {'rename_existing': 'BASE_PAUSE', ...} for
    # [gcode_macro pause]. Same fix applied to the RESUME side below (not yet
    # separately live-tested, but identical mechanism -- pause_resume.py
    # backs both PAUSE and RESUME the same way).
    {%- macro _resolve_pause_macro() -%}
        {%- set pause_cfg = printer.configfile.settings["gcode_macro pause"]|default({}) -%}
        {%- if pause_cfg.rename_existing -%}
            { pause_cfg.rename_existing }
        {%- else -%}
            {%- set _ = action_raise_error("Heatsoak: [gcode_macro PAUSE] has no rename_existing configured on this printer -- the real pause has nothing to call") -%}
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro _resolve_resume_macro() -%}
        {%- set resume_cfg = printer.configfile.settings["gcode_macro resume"]|default({}) -%}
        {%- if resume_cfg.rename_existing -%}
            { resume_cfg.rename_existing }
        {%- else -%}
            {%- set _ = action_raise_error("Heatsoak: [gcode_macro RESUME] has no rename_existing configured on this printer -- the real resume has nothing to call") -%}
        {%- endif -%}
    {%- endmacro -%}

    {%- macro resume_and_start() -%}
        UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=0
        { ui("end") }
        SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=heatsoak_active VALUE=0
        { zero_soak_fans() }
        {% if printer.pause_resume.is_paused %}
            RESTORE_GCODE_STATE NAME=HEATSOAK_STATE MOVE=0
            SAVE_GCODE_STATE NAME=PAUSE_STATE
            { _resolve_resume_macro() }
        {% endif %}
        {action_respond_info("Heatsoak complete -- calling _START_PRINT")}
        # Decoded named fields, NOT the raw rawparams string -- see
        # CHANGELOG at the top of this file. Boss-specific: forwards TOOL/TOOL_TEMP/T{n}_TEMP
        # too, on top of the generic fields, since Boss's own _START_PRINT (sovol-macros.cfg)
        # needs them for multi-tool setup -- see the state.update() block in the INIT branch
        # above for where these are captured.
        _START_PRINT BED_TEMP={state.get("bed", 60)|int} EXTRUDER_TEMP={state.get("noz", 200)|int} TOOL={state.get("tool", 0)|int} TOOL_TEMP={state.get("tool_temp", 200)|int} T0_TEMP={state.get("t0_temp", 0)|int} T1_TEMP={state.get("t1_temp", 0)|int} T2_TEMP={state.get("t2_temp", 0)|int} T3_TEMP={state.get("t3_temp", 0)|int} T4_TEMP={state.get("t4_temp", 0)|int} T5_TEMP={state.get("t5_temp", 0)|int} LAYER={state.get("layer", "")} FILAMENT={state.get("filament", "")} EXCLUDE={state.get("exclude", "")} SURFACE="{state.get('surface', '')}" OAPA={state.get("oapa", "")} DMGCC="{state.get('dmgcc', '')}"
        {% set _ = state.clear() %}
    {%- endmacro -%}

    # =========================================================================
    #  ADJUST BRANCH
    # =========================================================================
    {% if IS_ADJUST %}
        {% if state and (state|string) != "{}" and active %}
            {% set delta   = params.ADJUST|float * 60.0 %}
            {% set now     = printer.toolhead.estimated_print_time|float %}
            {% set newleft = [[remaining()|float + delta, 0]|max,
                               cfg.soak_max_seconds|float]|min %}
            {% set _ = state.update({"soak_deadline": now + newleft}) %}
            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{state}"
            { show_ui(newleft|int) }
            {% if not state.get("heating", false) %}
                UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=0.2
            {% endif %}
        {% else %}
            {action_respond_info("Heatsoak ADJUST ignored: no active soak")}
        {% endif %}

    # =========================================================================
    #  SKIP BRANCH
    # =========================================================================
    {% elif IS_SKIP %}
        {% if state and (state|string) != "{}" and active %}
            {action_respond_info("Heatsoak SKIP: proceeding to print")}
            { resume_and_start() }
        {% else %}
            {action_respond_info("Heatsoak SKIP ignored: no active soak")}
        {% endif %}

    # =========================================================================
    #  CANCEL BRANCH
    # =========================================================================
    {% elif IS_CANCEL %}
        {% if state and (state|string) != "{}" and active %}
            {action_respond_info("Heatsoak CANCEL")}
            UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=0
            UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_PREP DURATION=0
            UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK DURATION=0
            { ui("end") }
            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=heatsoak_active VALUE=0
            { zero_soak_fans() }
            RESTORE_GCODE_STATE NAME=HEATSOAK_STATE MOVE=0
            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{{}}"
            CANCEL_PRINT
        {% else %}
            {action_respond_info("Heatsoak CANCEL ignored: no active soak")}
        {% endif %}

    # =========================================================================
    #  HEATUP TICK BRANCH  --  non-blocking bed/nozzle heat-wait poll, with
    #  live popup updates. Replaces M190/M109 (see 2026-08-31 CHANGELOG and
    #  the comment in _HEATSOAK_PREP) -- each tick independently checks the
    #  current temp against target and either re-arms itself (still
    #  heating) or, once both are reached, parks and hands off to the real
    #  countdown heartbeat -- exactly what _HEATSOAK_PREP used to do inline
    #  via M190/M109/PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK. Tolerance is intentionally
    #  looser than Klipper's own PID-settle wait (which also checks the
    #  temperature's rate of change, not just its value) -- close enough
    #  for a pre-print soak target, not worth replicating exactly here.
    #  Tick interval is cfg.heatup_tick_interval (default 30s), not
    #  something shorter -- each show_ui() call resends a full
    #  action:prompt_begin/text/show sequence (Mainsail's popup protocol has
    #  no partial-update command), which rebuilds the whole dialog every
    #  time. At 2s that rebuild was fast enough to make the popup visibly
    #  flash/disappear before you could read the new number; 4s was still
    #  too fast to read comfortably (both found live 2026-08-31) -- 30s
    #  gives plenty of time to actually read it. Edit
    #  _HEATSOAK_CFG.heatup_tick_interval, not this branch, to change it.
    # =========================================================================
    {% elif IS_HEATUP_TICK %}
        {% if state and (state|string) != "{}" and active and state.get("heating", false) %}
            {% set bed_target = state.get("bed", 0)|float %}
            {% set bed_ready  = printer.heater_bed.temperature >= (bed_target - 1.0) %}

            {% if not bed_ready %}
                { show_ui(state.get("soak_total", 0)|float|int) }
                UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_HEATUP_TICK DURATION={cfg.heatup_tick_interval}
            {% else %}
                # Bed is at temp: start the soak clock right now, for the FULL configured
                # duration -- fixed, independent of whatever calibration below takes -- and
                # only then run calibration, in the same breath, instead of gating the
                # countdown behind it.
                #
                # IMPORTANT Klipper constraint (verified against klippy/extras/gcode_macro.py
                # GetStatusWrapper): a gcode_macro's ENTIRE Jinja template is rendered into one
                # flat gcode string up front -- printer.toolhead.estimated_print_time is read
                # once and CACHED for every reference within that one render -- and only after
                # the whole string is produced does Klipper dispatch it for real, line by line,
                # which is when real time actually elapses. That means a Jinja-level "how much
                # time did that take" check placed after PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK in THIS SAME
                # template call cannot see calibration's real duration -- it evaluates before
                # calibration ever runs for real, not after. So there is no same-call "resume
                # instantly the moment calibration finishes" branch here; that would be dead
                # code that always reads noticeably positive minutes remaining regardless of
                # calibration's real length. Also why the popup can't redraw live during
                # PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK -- it's committed to the string before calibration's
                # gcode even starts executing.
                #
                # What DOES work: the deadline itself is a real timestamp, correctly set below
                # BEFORE calibration's gcode runs. Once calibration actually finishes and this
                # call's remaining lines execute for real, the short heartbeat armed just below
                # fires moments later as a genuinely SEPARATE macro call/render -- which reads a
                # fresh, current estimated_print_time and correctly sees however long
                # calibration actually took. That's where "resume immediately if calibration
                # alone ran past the deadline" actually happens -- a few real seconds after
                # calibration returns, not synchronously with it. Kept short (1s, not the usual
                # 30s heatup_tick_interval) specifically to make that gap negligible.
                {% set _ = state.update({
                    "heating":       false,
                    "soak_deadline": printer.toolhead.estimated_print_time|float + state.get("soak_total", 0)|float,
                }) %}
                SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{state}"
                RESPOND TYPE=echo MSG="Heatsoak: bed at {bed_target}C. Countdown started, calibrating tools now."

                # CALIBRATE_TOOL_OFFSETS/KOMB don't need this gate to already be past any
                # particular nozzle temp -- both manage their own heating internally
                # (tools_calibrate.cfg's own M104/M109, KOMB.cfg's own
                # SET_HEATER_TEMPERATURE/TEMPERATURE_WAIT).
                PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK
                {% set _ = state.update({"calibrated": true}) %}
                SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{state}"
                UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=1
            {% endif %}
        {% endif %}

    # =========================================================================
    #  TICK BRANCH  --  heartbeat
    # =========================================================================
    {% elif IS_TICK %}
        {% if state and (state|string) != "{}" and active %}

            # -- Chamber wait check -------------------------------------------
            {% set chamber_sensor  = cfg.chamber_sensor|string|trim %}
            {% set chamber_target  = state.get("chamber_target", 0)|float %}
            {% set chamber_wait    = cfg.chamber_wait_temp|float %}
            {% set chamber_ready   = true %}

            {% if chamber_sensor and chamber_wait > 0 and chamber_target > 0 %}
                {% set ctemp_str = get_chamber_temp(chamber_sensor) %}
                {% if ctemp_str and ctemp_str|float < chamber_target %}
                    {% set chamber_ready = false %}
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}

            {% if not chamber_ready %}
                {% set ctemp_str = get_chamber_temp(chamber_sensor) %}
                {action_respond_info("Heatsoak: waiting for chamber -- %sC / %sC target" % (
                    (ctemp_str|float)|round(1)|string, chamber_target|int|string))}
                { show_ui(remaining()|float|int) }
                UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION=30
            {% else %}
                {% set soak_left = remaining()|float %}

                {% if soak_left <= 0 %}
                    { resume_and_start() }
                {% else %}
                    {% if soak_left > 300 %}
                        {% set interval = 60 %}
                    {% elif soak_left > 60 %}
                        {% set interval = 30 %}
                    {% elif soak_left > 10 %}
                        {% set interval = 10 %}
                    {% else %}
                        {% set interval = 5 %}
                    {% endif %}

                    { show_ui(soak_left|int) }
                    UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_UPDATE DURATION={interval}
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}

        {% endif %}

    # =========================================================================
    #  INIT BRANCH  --  called by slicer
    # =========================================================================
    {% elif IS_INIT %}

        {% set bed     = params.BED|default(params.BED_TEMP|default(0))|float %}
        {% set noz     = params.EXTRUDER|default(params.EXTRUDER_TEMP|default(params.TOOL_TEMP|default(200)))|float %}
        {% set chamber = params.CHAMBER|default(params.CHAMBER_TEMP|default(0))|float %}

        {action_respond_info("START_PRINT: bed=%sC  nozzle=%sC  chamber=%sC  file=%s" % (
            bed|string, noz|string, chamber|string,
            printer.print_stats.filename|default("unknown")))}

        # -- Parse print duration from filename -------------------------------
        {% set fname   = printer.print_stats.filename|default("")|string %}
        {% set dur_sec = get_duration_from_name(fname)|int %}
        {% set dur_min = (dur_sec / 60.0) if dur_sec >= 0 else cfg.soak_default_minutes|float %}

        # -- Chamber bypass check ---------------------------------------------
        {% set chamber_sensor  = cfg.chamber_sensor|string|trim %}
        {% set skip_within     = cfg.skip_if_chamber_within|float %}
        {% set chamber_is_hot  = false %}

        {% if chamber_sensor and skip_within > 0 and chamber > 0 %}
            {% set ctemp_str = get_chamber_temp(chamber_sensor) %}
            {% if ctemp_str and ctemp_str|float >= (chamber - skip_within) %}
                {% set chamber_is_hot = true %}
            {% endif %}
        {% endif %}

        # -- Should we soak? --------------------------------------------------
        {% set should_soak = (
            not chamber_is_hot
            and bed >= cfg.min_bed_temp_to_trigger|float
            and dur_min >= cfg.min_print_minutes_to_trigger|float
        ) %}

        {action_respond_info("Heatsoak decision: should_soak=%s  dur_min=%s  chamber_hot=%s" % (
            should_soak|string, dur_min|round(1)|string, chamber_is_hot|string))}

        {% if not should_soak %}
            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=heatsoak_active VALUE=0
            # No START_PRINT_BASE/MMU section on Boss -- calls straight into Boss's own
            # _START_PRINT (sovol-macros.cfg), forwarding the raw slicer params unchanged
            # (this path is synchronous, so nothing needs to survive in `state`).
            _START_PRINT {rawparams}

        {% else %}
            {% set raw_soak = cfg.soak_sec_per_print_min|float * dur_min %}
            {% set soak_sec = [[raw_soak, cfg.soak_min_seconds|float]|max,
                                cfg.soak_max_seconds|float]|min %}

            # Store decoded named fields, not the slicer's raw {rawparams}
            # string -- see CHANGELOG at the top of this file for why. Add
            # whatever named params your own slicer profile passes.
            #
            # Boss-specific: TOOL/TOOL_TEMP/T0_TEMP../CALIBRATE are needed by Boss's own
            # _START_PRINT and PRINTER_HEATSOAK_PARK (see both below) -- the generic template
            # doesn't know about these on its own, so they're captured here explicitly, the
            # same way LAYER/FILAMENT/etc. already are.
            {% set _ = state.update({
                "bed":            bed|float,
                "noz":            noz|float,
                "soak_total":     soak_sec|float,
                "heating":        true,
                "calibrated":     false,
                "chamber_target": chamber|float,
                "print_dur_sec":  dur_sec|int,
                "layer":          params.LAYER|default("")|string,
                "filament":       params.FILAMENT|default("")|string,
                "exclude":        params.EXCLUDE|default("")|string,
                "surface":        params.SURFACE|default("")|string,
                "oapa":           params.OAPA|default("")|string,
                "dmgcc":          params.DMGCC|default("")|string,
                "tool":           params.TOOL|default(0)|int,
                "tool_temp":      params.TOOL_TEMP|default(noz)|int,
                "calibrate":      params.CALIBRATE|default(1)|int,
                "t0_temp":        params.T0_TEMP|default(0)|int,
                "t1_temp":        params.T1_TEMP|default(0)|int,
                "t2_temp":        params.T2_TEMP|default(0)|int,
                "t3_temp":        params.T3_TEMP|default(0)|int,
                "t4_temp":        params.T4_TEMP|default(0)|int,
                "t5_temp":        params.T5_TEMP|default(0)|int,
            }) %}

            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=heatsoak_active VALUE=1
            SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO=START_PRINT VARIABLE=state VALUE="{state}"

            SAVE_GCODE_STATE NAME=HEATSOAK_STATE

            # Start bed heating (don't block yet)
            M140 S{bed}

            # Home if needed
            {% if printer.toolhead.homed_axes|lower != "xyz" %}
                G28
            {% endif %}

            # THIS IS WHAT ACTUALLY BLOCKS THE PRINT DURING THE SOAK.
            # Everything above and below this line (popup, countdown,
            # delayed_gcode timers) is purely cosmetic without it: this
            # macro's gcode block simply returns once it reaches this point,
            # and Klipper immediately continues executing the NEXT lines in
            # the sliced file (the real print body) regardless of
            # heatsoak_active/soak_left. This calls Klipper's real
            # pause_resume.pause(), matched by _resolve_resume_macro() in
            # resume_and_start() above once the soak completes or Skip is hit.
            #
            # The actual macro name (BASE_PAUSE vs PAUSE_BASE, depending on
            # your [gcode_macro PAUSE]'s rename_existing) is auto-detected by
            # _resolve_pause_macro() above -- no manual verification needed
            # before deploying anymore. If neither name exists on this
            # printer it hard-errors here instead of silently doing nothing,
            # unlike the old hardcoded-name version: a wrong/missing name
            # used to fail as a silent "Unknown command" that let a real
            # layer print underneath a "running" soak on the Voron 0,
            # 2026-08-04/05. Matches the community-standard "interruptible
            # heat soak" pattern (Klipper Discourse topic 1552).
            { _resolve_pause_macro() }

            # Show popup immediately, BEFORE machine starts moving
            { show_ui(soak_sec|int) }

            # Build-plate SVG layout -- sent once to the Console tab (not the
            # popup, see draw_buildplate_layout_svg() above for why), right
            # alongside the popup so it's available to check during the soak.
            {% if printer.exclude_object is defined and printer.exclude_object.objects %}
                {action_respond_info(draw_buildplate_layout_svg(
                    printer.exclude_object.objects,
                    printer.toolhead.axis_maximum.x|float,
                    printer.toolhead.axis_maximum.y|float))}
            {% endif %}

            # Physical prep fires 0.1s later so popup renders first. Prep
            # itself schedules the countdown heartbeat once bed/nozzle/park
            # are actually done -- see CHANGELOG (2026-08-27) and the note
            # at the end of [delayed_gcode _HEATSOAK_PREP].
            UPDATE_DELAYED_GCODE ID=_HEATSOAK_PREP DURATION=0.1
        {% endif %}

    {% endif %}

    SET_GCODE_VARIABLE MACRO={SELF} VARIABLE=state VALUE="{state}"


